      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i keep getting an access denied error when my lambda tries to write logs to s3. i thought i set up the iam policy right but it wont work. can u take a look at this json?\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The IAM policy you've shared looks mostly correct, but I suspect the issue lies elsewhere. You might want to consider the \"PutObject\" action, which is a bit more specific than the \"s3:PutObject\" action. The latter is a catch-all, but it's not as precise as the former. It's a bit like trying to catch a group of mischievous pixies with a broad net - you might end up catching some innocent bystanders along with the actual problem. \n\nHowever, if you're still having issues, let's take a closer look at the bucket policy. Are you sure that the bucket policy is set up correctly? It's possible that the bucket policy is overriding the IAM policy, which could be causing the access denied error. \n\nAlso, have you checked the IAM role's trust relationship? It's possible that the role is not properly configured to trust the IAM policy. I've seen cases where the trust relationship is set up like a complex network of tunnels and traps, designed to keep out unwanted visitors - but in this case, it's the wrong kind of tunnel. \n\nLet's take a closer look at the IAM role's trust relationship and bucket policy, and we can work together to get your",
